5:[["$","$L10","2.10",{"topic":{"id":"2.10","title":"Section 63(4) BSA and Electronic Evidence Certification","minutes":18,"summary":"Prepare a complete, truthful certificate worksheet that identifies the output, its source, the process, and the people able to explain it.","sections":[{"title":"The statutory conditions","body":["Section 63(2) addresses regular use of the computer or communication device by a person with lawful control, regular feeding of the relevant kind of information, proper operation during the material period, and derivation of the output from that information. Where operation was interrupted or faulty, the question includes whether the fault affected the electronic record or its accuracy.","Section 63(3) addresses devices used singly, in a system or network, or in combination. Identify all materially relevant sources rather than describing only the final display device. For cloud-hosted records, the export workstation and the service generating the record may play different roles. The explanation must match the actual process, not a generic assertion that “the computer was working”."]},{"title":"Part A and Part B","body":["Subsection (4) requires a certificate identifying the electronic record, describing how it was produced, giving appropriate device particulars, and addressing the conditions. It is to be signed by the person in charge of the device or relevant activities and an expert, in the Schedule’s form, at each instance of submission for admission.","Part A is completed by the party and identifies the source, make and model, serial or other identifiers, lawful control, ordinary operation, and hash particulars. Part B is completed by the expert and addresses the source and technical particulars, including the hash and algorithm. Attach the hash report. A person must not sign an expert portion merely because a form has a blank space; the roles and knowledge need to be genuine."]},{"title":"Practical preparation and review","body":["Preserve the exact output being submitted and calculate its hash using a documented algorithm. SHA-256 is a sensible integrity choice for this course; the Schedule lists several algorithms and an appropriate other standard. Record file names, sizes, acquisition or export steps, device identifiers, time zone, tool versions, and relevant limitations. Keep the hash report consistent with the final paginated or exported output.","A certificate does not cure unlawful collection, false content, or an unexplained custody gap. Check who can competently sign each part and whether multiple devices or outputs require additional explanation. Obtain the current official Schedule and legal review before filing. The interactive worksheet deliberately remains marked as training material and cannot substitute for the required signatures."]}],"practice":{"title":"Prepare the invoice certificate worksheet","scenario":"A lawfully controlled laptop produced an exported invoice PDF and a SHA-256 report.","steps":["Identify the exact PDF, source device, and lawful controller.","Complete the party’s details and ordinary-operation explanation.","Add the expert’s actual particulars and attach the hash report.","Review both parts against the official Schedule and mark the classroom copy as training only."],"debrief":"The certificate describes a real process. Accurate particulars and competent signatories matter more than polished wording copied from an unrelated case."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bsa","iea"],"sources":["bsa","bsa-schedule","arjun"],"labId":"certificate","unit":2,"objectives":["Explain the statutory conditions and apply it to an investigation.","Explain part A and Part B and apply it to an investigation.","Explain practical preparation and review and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-2.10","prompt":"Who signs the BSA Schedule certificate?","options":["Only the advocate, in every case.","The party and an expert in their respective parts.","Only the police officer who receives it.","No one; an unsigned hash is always sufficient."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Schedule provides Part A for the party and Part B for the expert, reflecting section 63(4).","unit":2}}}]]
